---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { apps } from '../data/apps';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What alexmerced.app is, how the apps store your data, how to move it between browsers, and what happens if you clear your browsing data."
  keywords={['local first', 'indexeddb', 'browser storage', 'data export']}
>
  <article class="about">
    <div class="shell about__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>{apps.length} tools, local data</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        This is a shelf of small utilities I wanted for myself. They are free, they need no account,
        and your work is processed in your browser. Storage and optional downloads have a few
        limits worth understanding before you put real work into one of them.
      </p>

      <h2>Where your data actually lives</h2>
      <p>
        Apps use <strong>IndexedDB</strong> or <strong>localStorage</strong> for saved work and preferences;
        some file converters keep their inputs only in memory. Browser storage is scoped to this
        domain, in this browser, on this device. There is no copy anywhere else, because there is
        nowhere else.
      </p>
      <p>What follows from that:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Opening the same app in a different browser shows an empty start. Nothing is missing, it is simply somewhere else.</li>
        <li>Private or incognito windows usually discard everything when you close them.</li>
        <li>Clearing site data, cookies, or "browsing data" in your browser settings will delete what these apps have stored, and there is no way for me to recover it.</li>
        <li>Nobody, me included, can see what you write here.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>So export things you care about</h2>
      <p>
        Workspace apps offer JSON export and import. File tools download their results in formats
        such as PDF, WAV, SVG or video. Limelight can save edits alone or a project with its
        recordings. Keep a downloaded copy of work you want to preserve.
      </p>
      <p>
        Import asks whether you want to <em>merge</em>, which keeps what is already there and layers
        the file on top, or <em>replace</em>, which clears first. Merging resolves conflicts by
        keeping whichever copy was edited most recently. Both refuse a file that came from a
        different app. Invalid records may be repaired or dropped; an unreadable workspace is refused.
      </p>
      <p>Use it to move between browsers, to back up before you experiment, or simply to hold your own copy.</p>

      <h2>The apps</h2>
      <dl class="about__apps">
        {apps.map((app) => (
          <div>
            <dt><a href={`/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></dt>
            <dd>{app.description}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <h2>Installing it</h2>
      <p>
        The site is a progressive web app, so your browser can install it to your home screen or
        dock and open it in its own window. On Android and desktop Chrome an <strong>Install</strong>
        button appears in the header when the browser offers it. On an iPhone, use Share, then
        <em>Add to Home Screen</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the offline cache finishes installing, the core pages and tools work without a connection.
        Quarry’s SQL engine and Limelight’s optional transcription downloads must be loaded online
        before use; their offline availability depends on the browser cache. An
        installed copy shares its storage with the same browser, so what you write in one appears
        in the other.
      </p>

      <h2>How they are built</h2>
      <p>
        Static HTML and CSS with plain TypeScript, generated by Astro. No UI framework, no
        analytics, and no externally loaded fonts. Quarry uses self-hosted DuckDB. Limelight’s optional
        transcription downloads Transformers.js from jsDelivr and speech models from Hugging Face
        after you request it; audio processing happens locally. The logic that
        matters, the expression parser in Reckoner, the page tree in Warren, the card movement in
        Laneway, and the QR encoder in Tessera, is covered by a test suite that runs on every change.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tessera's QR encoder is written from scratch rather than pulled from a library: it implements
        the specification's numeric, alphanumeric, and byte modes across all forty versions and four
        error correction levels, with Reed-Solomon over GF(2^8) and all eight data masks scored by
        the four penalty rules. Its tests read the finished matrix back and check that what comes out
        is what went in.
      </p>

      <h2>Bugs and suggestions</h2>
      <p>
        The source is on <a href="https://github.com/AlexMercedCoder/alexmerced-app">GitHub</a>. If
        something is broken, an issue there is the surest way to reach me. I am
        <a href="https://alexmerced.com">Alex Merced</a>, and I write about data and AI in rather
        more depth elsewhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about { padding: 3rem 0 4rem; }
  .about__inner { max-width: 42rem; }
  .about h1 { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  .about h2 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin: 2.5rem 0 0.7rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--text-soft); }
  .about p { color: var(--text-soft); }
  .about ul { color: var(--text-soft); padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .about a { color: var(--accent); }

  .about__apps { display: grid; gap: 1rem; margin: 1rem 0 0; }
  .about__apps dt { font-weight: 700; }
  .about__apps dt a { text-decoration: none; }
  .about__apps dt a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .about__apps dd { margin: 0.2rem 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-soft); }
</style>
